===Etymology 1===
{{bor+|en|frm|rapiere}}, from {{der|en|frm|([[espee]]) [[rapiere]]}}, from {{der|en|fro|rapiere}}, {{m|fro|raspiere}}, from {{der|en|es|raspadera|t=poker; raker; scraper}}, from {{der|en|es|raspar|t=to scrape}}, of {{uder|en|gem|-}} origin. More at {{l|en|rasp}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈɹeɪpˌɪə(ɹ)/}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[slender]], [[straight]], [[sharp]]ly [[pointed]] [[sword]] (double-[[edge]]d, single-edged or edgeless).
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet}}, act IV scene 1
#*: […] In his lawless fit,
#*: Behind the arras hearing something stir,
#*: Whips out his '''rapier''', cries ‘A rat, a rat!’
#*: And in this brainish apprehension kills
#*: The unseen good old man.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1911|author={{w|G. K. Chesterton}}|title=''The Innocence of Father Brown''| chapter=The Sins of Prince Saradine|passage=The man beside him with the earrings and the big black case proceeded to unlock it. He took out of it two long Italian '''rapiers''', with splendid steel hilts and blades, which he planted point downwards in the lawn.}}

